### Health check
GET http://localhost:8080/api/health


### Get all assets from MongoDB
GET http://localhost:8080/api/assets


### Get one asset
# Replace the ID below with an actual ID returned by GET /api/assets
GET http://localhost:8080/api/assets/6a50976fdc292efb8000ad0e


### Get missing asset
GET http://localhost:8080/api/assets/000000000000000000000000


### Get all tickets from MongoDB
GET http://localhost:8080/api/tickets


### Get one ticket
# Replace the ID below with an actual ID returned by GET /api/tickets
GET http://localhost:8080/api/tickets/6a506b3be6726dbbf375755e


### Get missing ticket
GET http://localhost:8080/api/tickets/000000000000000000000000


### Create a valid asset
POST http://localhost:8080/api/assets
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "assetTag": "TAB-2026-001",
  "name": "Samsung Galaxy Tab",
  "category": "Tablet",
  "serialNumber": "SN-TAB-001",
  "location": "HQ Level 4"
}


### Create an invalid asset
POST http://localhost:8080/api/assets
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "assetTag": "",
  "name": "",
  "category": "",
  "serialNumber": "",
  "location": ""
}

### How to confirmed the data came from MongoDB
first check the tickets collection in MongoDB Compass under support_desk_db. 
The ticket document had the MongoDB _id value 6a506b3be6726dbbf375755e.

Then I called GET http://localhost:8080/api/tickets in the Spring Boot API. 
The response returned the same ticket data and the same MongoDB id. 
This confirms the API is reading ticket data from MongoDB through TicketRepository, 
not from a hardcoded in-memory list.